From: Aron Xu <aron@debian.org>
Date: Tue, 20 May 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: zap_leaf: bound chunk-chain walks in lookup and lookup_closest

zap_leaf_lookup_closest() (readdir / fzap_cursor_retrieve) and
zap_leaf_lookup() (open / zap_lookup) both walk a per-bucket chunk
chain by following le->le_next with no termination guard.  If the
leaf's l_hash[] entry points at a freed chunk, or the chain forms a
cycle, the loop runs forever and the calling CPU appears as a kernel
soft lockup.  The ASSERT3U() guards inside the loops are debug-only
and compile to no-ops in production builds.

Observed in the field on a Debian 6.12.86 host running zfs-linux
2.3.2.  Two unrelated processes (updatedb.plocate, claude) wedged on
the same leaf object (matching kernel pointers in R14/RDI) at the same
chunk index across watchdog samples taken seconds apart, with stack:

    zap_leaf_lookup_closest+0x9X..0x12X
    fzap_cursor_retrieve+0x197
    zap_cursor_retrieve+0x216
    zfs_readdir+0x219
    zpl_iterate+0x53
    __x64_sys_getdents64

After installing the readdir-side guard, the warning emitted on the
corrupted leaf was:

    ZAP leaf blkid=162: chunk 0 has type 253 (expected 252); pool may
    be corrupt

i.e. l_hash[lh] of leaf 162 was pointing at chunk 0, but chunk 0 was
on the freelist (ZAP_CHUNK_FREE).  The block's on-disk checksum is
valid, scrub finds no errors -- the corruption is logical and lives
inside an encrypted leaf whose MAC matches.  Two distinct directories
in the same dataset exhibit the same pattern: a 10.6MB ZAP that has
been static since dataset creation (all 647 leaves at low txg) and a
4.2MB ZAP that has been rewritten recently (all 257 leaves at high
txg) -- so the responsible write-path defect has reproduced more than
once across the dataset's life.  Because the bad leaf is reachable
from open() as well as readdir(), guarding only the latter still
allowed lookup to hang.

Harden both walks identically by:
 - counting visited chunks; if the count reaches ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l)
   without hitting CHAIN_END we must be cycling,
 - validating that each chunk index is in range before dereferencing,
 - validating that le_type == ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY before use.

On any of these, log via cmn_err(CE_WARN) with the leaf blkid and
return EIO.  Callers already propagate non-zero, non-ENOENT errors
back to userspace, so the affected syscall (getdents64, openat, etc.)
surfaces EIO instead of wedging the CPU.  This turns a hang into a
clean error; it does not repair the underlying corruption -- the
affected directories must be moved aside and recreated.

The bound is correct: a leaf contains at most ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l)
chunks, so any chain that visits more chunks than that without
terminating must have revisited at least one chunk.

Downstream defensive change; upstream has no fix across any released
branch up to and including 2.4.2.  Both functions are unchanged from
their initial Linux import apart from the file-split refactor in
8ff64005a.

Forwarded: not-needed
Author: Aron Xu <aron@debian.org>
Last-Update: 2026-05-20
---
Index: zfs/module/zfs/zap_leaf.c
===================================================================
--- zfs.orig/module/zfs/zap_leaf.c
+++ zfs/module/zfs/zap_leaf.c
@@ -382,14 +382,43 @@ int
 zap_leaf_lookup(zap_leaf_t *l, zap_name_t *zn, zap_entry_handle_t *zeh)
 {
 	struct zap_leaf_entry *le;
+	uint_t nchunks = ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l);
+	uint_t visited = 0;
 
 	ASSERT3U(zap_leaf_phys(l)->l_hdr.lh_magic, ==, ZAP_LEAF_MAGIC);
 
 	for (uint16_t *chunkp = LEAF_HASH_ENTPTR(l, zn->zn_hash);
 	    *chunkp != CHAIN_END; chunkp = &le->le_next) {
 		uint16_t chunk = *chunkp;
+		if (chunk >= nchunks) {
+			cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+			    "chunk index %u out of range (max %u) "
+			    "during lookup; pool may be corrupt",
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid,
+			    (uint_t)chunk, nchunks);
+			return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+		}
+		if (visited++ >= nchunks) {
+			cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+			    "chunk chain cycle detected during lookup; "
+			    "pool may be corrupt",
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid);
+			return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+		}
 		le = ZAP_LEAF_ENTRY(l, chunk);
 
+		if (le->le_type != ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY) {
+			cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+			    "chunk %u has type %u (expected %u) "
+			    "during lookup; pool may be corrupt",
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+			    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid, (uint_t)chunk,
+			    (uint_t)le->le_type,
+			    (uint_t)ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY);
+			return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+		}
 		ASSERT3U(chunk, <, ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l));
 		ASSERT3U(le->le_type, ==, ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY);
 
@@ -429,15 +458,44 @@ zap_leaf_lookup_closest(zap_leaf_t *l,
 	uint64_t besth = -1ULL;
 	uint32_t bestcd = -1U;
 	uint16_t bestlh = ZAP_LEAF_HASH_NUMENTRIES(l)-1;
+	uint_t nchunks = ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l);
 	struct zap_leaf_entry *le;
 
 	ASSERT3U(zap_leaf_phys(l)->l_hdr.lh_magic, ==, ZAP_LEAF_MAGIC);
 
 	for (uint16_t lh = LEAF_HASH(l, h); lh <= bestlh; lh++) {
+		uint_t visited = 0;
 		for (uint16_t chunk = zap_leaf_phys(l)->l_hash[lh];
 		    chunk != CHAIN_END; chunk = le->le_next) {
+			if (chunk >= nchunks) {
+				cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+				    "chunk index %u out of range (max %u) "
+				    "at lh=%u; pool may be corrupt",
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid,
+				    (uint_t)chunk, nchunks, (uint_t)lh);
+				return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+			}
+			if (visited++ >= nchunks) {
+				cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+				    "chunk chain cycle detected at lh=%u; "
+				    "pool may be corrupt",
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid, (uint_t)lh);
+				return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+			}
 			le = ZAP_LEAF_ENTRY(l, chunk);
 
+			if (le->le_type != ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY) {
+				cmn_err(CE_WARN, "ZAP object=%llu leaf blkid=%llu: "
+				    "chunk %u has type %u (expected %u); "
+				    "pool may be corrupt",
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_dbuf->db_object,
+				    (u_longlong_t)l->l_blkid, (uint_t)chunk,
+				    (uint_t)le->le_type,
+				    (uint_t)ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY);
+				return (SET_ERROR(EIO));
+			}
 			ASSERT3U(chunk, <, ZAP_LEAF_NUMCHUNKS(l));
 			ASSERT3U(le->le_type, ==, ZAP_CHUNK_ENTRY);
 
